      "title": "5.3-magnitude earthquake hits China's Qinghai days after Tibet quake",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/5-3-magnitude-earthquake-hits-china-s-qinghai-days-after-tibet-quake-126102400849_1.html",
      "description": "A 5.3-magnitude earthquake struck China's Qinghai province on Saturday, two days after a powerful 7.0-magnitude temblor in neighbouring Tibet left 16 people dead and 76 others injured.\nThe latest quake jolted Zadoi County in northwest China's Qinghai Province at 4:39 pm (Beijing Time), according to the China Earthquake Networks Centre (CENC).\nThe epicentre, at a depth of 10 kilometres, was monitored at 32.54 degrees north latitude and 93.97 degrees east longitude, the centre said.\nThere were no immediate reports of casualties or damage from Saturday's earthquake.\nThe fragile Tibetan Plateau is prone to powerful earthquakes as it lies in a region where the Indian and Eurasian tectonic plates collide, generating significant seismic activity.\nMeanwhile, rescue and relief operations continued after the 7.0-magnitude earthquake struck Baqen County in Nagqu city of the Tibet Autonomous Region at 10:33 pm (local time) on Thursday.\nSo far, 12,489 residents have been evacuated, and rescue",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-24T12:40:28"
    },
    {
      "title": "Rescuers search for survivors after Tibet earthquake kills 16, injures 76",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/rescuers-search-for-survivors-after-tibet-earthquake-kills-16-injures-76-126102400065_1.html",
      "description": "Rescuers combed through a remote area in the Chinese region of Tibet on Friday after a magnitude 7 earthquake killed at least 16 people, injured 76 and collapsed hundreds of homes, according to Chinese state media.\nThe disaster struck Nagqu, a city north of Tibet's capital Lhasa that is some 4,800 metres (15,748 feet) above sea level, on Thursday. At least 339 homes collapsed, and officials told journalists they were concerned about further building collapses from aftershocks, as well as casualties from rockslides.\nChina's national broadcaster CCTV aired footage on Friday showing collapsed houses and shattered stone walls in Nagqu.\nMore than 12,000 residents in the region were evacuated, with additional shelters under construction, China's official news agency Xinhua reported. No one was reported missing in Baqen County in Nagqu, where the earthquake struck.\nSearch and rescue efforts are complicated by the geography and low temperatures, officials said. As of Friday afternoon, ...",
